raw:
{
  "slots": {
    "question": [
      {
        "claim": "How did partisan polarization over lockdowns, masks, and vaccines vary across U.S. states and Canadian provinces, and how did it change around particular events?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Our work addresses this need by providing a comprehensive, large-scale study of geographic and event-driven variation in online partisan polarization surrounding COVID-19 discussions across American states and Canadian provinces.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "Our analysis focused on three specific pandemic-related topics: lockdowns, masks, and vaccines.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "How is online partisan polarization related to regional political leanings, conspiracy-related content, and vaccination rates?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Specifically, we analyze millions of\ntweets to determine how polarization correlates with: (1) the ideological leanings of different regions; (2) exposure to conspiracy theory-related content; and (3) vaccination rates.",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "How can a text-based measure distinguish partisan polarization from ordinary differences in how social and demographic groups speak?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the language of political discussions across socio-demographic groups naturally varies, with each\ngroup having their own lexicon [40]. Therefore, dissimilar language alone does not necessarily indicate polarized\npositions.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "central_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Pandemic discourse did not exhibit a single, uniform partisan divide: polarization varied by region, political leaning, and intervention, and the comparison suggests that local political and institutional conditions matter for how online divisions relate to health behavior.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "By analyzing millions of tweets and\ntracking semantic differences between partisan groups across different regions and topics, we have demonstrated that\npolarization is not uniform but varies significantly by geography, political leaning, and public health topic, with substantial\ndifferences between the two countries.",
            "page": 25
          },
          {
            "text": "This suggests that the impact of online polarization on public health behaviors may be mediated by broader political and institutional factors.",
            "page": 25
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors propose a scalable way to track partisan polarization at fine geographic, temporal, and topical resolution using differences in how partisans discuss the same issue; the approach is intended to be reusable beyond COVID-19.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We introduce a methodological framework to reliably measure polarization in public discourse at fine-grained resolution,\nleveraging the vast quantity of human-generated text available on digital platforms.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "While our study focuses on the COVID-19 context, the proposed\nmethodology for quantifying partisan polarization at scale can be adapted to examine large-scale discussions across\nvarious temporal, spatial, and topical dimensions.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "when examining discussions on the same contentious topic, language dissimilarity between different partisan\ngroups more likely reflects alternative semantic understandings of that specific topic—a phenomenon we hypothesize\ncorrelates strongly with polarization.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "evidence": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors used Twitter's official API to collect a 1% real-time sample from October 9, 2020, to January 4, 2021, then filtered it for political and COVID-19 content. The resulting corpus contained about 12.6 million U.S. tweets and 46.6 million Canadian tweets.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We used Twitter’s (X) official API to collect 1% of real-time tweets for the United States and\nCanada from October 9, 2020 to January 4, 2021.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "We filtered the data using political keywords:",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "This process yielded 12,552,213 tweets from 2,657,355 users for the United States and 46,636,206 tweets from\n1,757,675 users for Canada that shared COVID-19-related content.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Lockdowns, masks, and vaccines were selected as salient examples of interventions aimed at group behavior, individual behavior, and medical protection, respectively. A tweet could receive more than one topic label, and the conspiracy category counted both promotion and rebuttal.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "These topics were\nselected for their salience in polarized discourse [27,43,44], and because they represent different types of interventions\n(group behavior, individual behavior, and medicine, respectively).",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "For\nconspiracy-related tweets, relevant indicates that the content pertains to COVID-19 conspiracy theories, including tweets\nthat either promote the conspiracy or debunk / refute it, i.e., any tweet discussing the theory in either context. A tweet\ncould belong to multiple topics.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The topic-labeling pipeline used frequent hashtags annotated by two political scientists, supplemented by hashtags from prior studies. The authors adapted RoBERTa separately for each country and used manually reviewed tweets and lower-weight hashtag labels to train topic classifiers.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "1. We extracted all hashtags within our dataset, ordered by frequency, and discarded those appearing fewer than 100\ntimes (yielding 18,000 hashtags for the United States and 3,600 for Canada).\n2. Two political scientists manually annotated this list with topic and relevance labels, narrowing it to 631 relevant\nhashtags.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "We merged these with hashtags identified in previous studies for the same topics [45–47].",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "We then domain-adapted RoBERTa-base models [41] on the COVID-19 tweets from each country’s dataset through\nself-supervised learning (predicting masked words within tweets), creating country-specific pre-trained language\nmodels.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "Two political scientists independently reviewed each tweet to determine relevance, discarding\ntweets where consensus couldn’t be reached. We then fine-tuned the respective domain-adapted RoBERTa models to\nclassify by topics.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "During training, we also incorporated hashtag-annotated tweets as weak supervision, assigning them a reduced loss weight of 0.9 compared to 1.0 for\ngold-standard labels.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Users were geolocated from profile text only when Open Street Map and ArcGIS returned coordinates within one degree of one another. The regional user counts closely tracked census populations in both countries.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We processed this free-form text field using Open Street Map and the ArcGIS API, which both return latitude and\nlongitude coordinates when a location is found. We established a clear geolocation when both APIs returned coordinates\nwithin one degree of each other",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "In total, we successfully geolocated 757,601 U.S. users with a strong correlation of 0.98",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "to official population census data, and 282,454 Canadian users with a strong correlation of 0.92",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Party inference proceeded in two stages: a profile-text classifier identified affiliations among politically explicit users, and those labels trained an activity classifier using averaged tweet embeddings. The activity cutoffs were five COVID-related tweets in Canada and ten in the United States.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "1. Profile classifier: We first identified politically explicit users based on their profile descriptions, using partisan keywords for each country’s major parties.\n2. Activity classifier: We then used these profile-based classifications as labels to train a classifier based on user activity\npatterns.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "For the activity classifier, we generated user embeddings by aggregating tweet embeddings\nfrom each user (using mean pooling), then trained a multi-layer perceptron to predict party affiliation.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "We filtered users\nbased on their activity level (number of COVID-related tweets), with optimal thresholds determined to be 5 tweets for\nCanada and 10 tweets for the United States.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The U.S. comparison was between Democrats and Republicans. The Canadian comparison pooled Liberal, New Democratic, and Green supporters on the left and Conservative and People's Party supporters on the right.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "For the United States, we identified supporters of the Democratic (left) and Republican (right) parties. For Canada, we\ngrouped users into left (Liberal Party, New Democratic Party, and Green Party) and right (Conservative Party and People’s\nParty) partisan families.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors checked inferred affiliations against regional election results in both countries and against matched voter records in five U.S. states and Washington, D.C. The profile and activity classifiers agreed with the voter records about three-quarters of the time.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "For the U.S. and Canada, we calculated the correlation between the ratio of liberal to\nconservative-labeled users per region in our dataset compared to official election results, obtaining strong correlations of\n0.802 for the United States and 0.815 for Canada (visualized in Fig 3 and Fig 2).",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "For further external validation of our U.S. party affiliation\npredictions, we matched Twitter users with primary voter registration records available for five states (Ohio, New York,\nFlorida, Arkansas, North Carolina) and Washington DC.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "Our profile classifier achieved 74.35% accuracy and our activity classifier achieved 73.35% accuracy when compared\nto these official records.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors averaged relevant tweet embeddings into one vector per user for a given topic and used the C-index to score separation between partisan groups on a zero-to-one scale.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "To obtain a user embedding for a given topic, we aggregate all\nof the user’s relevant tweet embeddings by averaging.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "We then measure polarization by quantifying how far apart the language of left and right-leaning users is positioned\nin this embedding space, using the C-index [42], a robust clustering measure.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "This index ranges from 0 (no polarization) to 1 (extreme polarization where partisan groups are completely isolated from\neach other in the embedding space).",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "To make the index practical on large samples, the authors repeatedly subsampled users and increased the sample size until the estimated scores were stable. On daily lockdown and vaccine data, the approximation error fell below 0.001 at about 10,000 users.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Our algorithm (Algorithm 1) repeatedly samples a fraction of users, calculates the polarization index on this sample,\nand increases the sample size until the coefficient of variation falls below a specified threshold (e.g., 10 [48]).",
            "page": 11
          },
          {
            "text": "We validated our approximation method on daily lockdown and vaccine tweets. As shown in Fig 4, the absolute error\ndrops dramatically at around 3,000 users and falls below 0.001 at approximately 10,000 users.",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The regional comparisons averaged 12 weeks of polarization scores and used election vote margins to characterize political leaning. The national time series used daily scores and examined both events selected in advance and events identified by inspecting the largest peaks.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Color intensity from light to dark gives the amount of polarization measured weekly between October 11, 2020 to January 3, 2021 and then averaged\nover the 12 weeks.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "When examined against partisan voting patterns (using the 2020 U.S. presidential election and 2019 Canadian federal\nelection), we observe a clear trend: conservative states and provinces show higher levels of polarization compared\nto their liberal counterparts.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "In particular, we examined two types of events: 1) pre-selected political and\nvaccine-related events (Table 8, shown as vertical lines in Fig 13) and 2) highly polarized events detected through analysis of the highest peaks in polarization (Tables 9 and 10, shown as red circles in Fig 13).",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The health data came from official U.S. and Canadian sources. They included reported cases and deaths, and regional vaccination coverage one year after the Twitter observation period, measured as receipt of at least two doses.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We used officially reported COVID-19 new cases and death data from each country.\nFor the United States, we sourced data from the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC). For Canada, we use\nthe data reported by the Canadian government.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "We used officially reported vaccination rates from one year after our Twitter\ndata collection period, as COVID-19 vaccines were only created and approved at the very end of our initial data\ncollection. The vaccination rates represent those who obtained at least two doses.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "claim": "Conservative regions generally showed greater partisan polarization, but the relationship was clearer in the United States: Republican vote share correlated significantly with polarization over masks and vaccines, not lockdowns. The comparable Canadian associations were not statistically significant, and overall polarization was lower in Canada.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We find\nstrong, significant correlations in the US between Republican vote share and polarization around masks and vaccines,\nthough not for lockdowns. For Canada, these associations are not statistically significant, partly due to the smaller number\nof regions analyzed.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "Canada, while showing similar patterns, demonstrated significantly lower overall polarization levels.",
            "page": 25
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Regional rankings did not follow political leaning uniformly. In U.S. lockdown discussions, conservative Idaho, Alabama, and Arkansas were among the least polarized, while liberal Delaware ranked first. Quebec had Canada's highest polarization over vaccines.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The lockdown discourse shows a more mixed pattern, with outliers from both liberal\nand conservative states; namely Idaho, Alabama, and Arkansas showing the least polarization, and Delaware (ranked 1),\nColorado (ranked 17), and New Jersey (ranked 15) showing higher values.",
            "page": 14
          },
          {
            "text": "Delaware, a liberal state, exhibited unexpectedly high polarization",
            "page": 23
          },
          {
            "text": "Our results confirmed that Quebec had the highest\nlevel of partisan division over vaccines, alongside the highest reported COVID-19 incidence in Canada during the first\nand second pandemic waves [89].",
            "page": 24
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "U.S. states with more polarized vaccine discussions had lower subsequent vaccination rates (r = −0.77). Canada did not show the same negative relationship; an exploratory analysis excluding Nunavut instead found a positive correlation across 12 regions.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Using official CDC vaccination data from a comparable period after vaccines became\navailable (October 11, 2021 to January 3, 2022), we confirm a strong negative correlation (r = −0.77, p < 0.001) between\nvaccine polarization and vaccination rates.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "We remove Nunavut as an outlier because of its very small population. The correlation is 0.74 with CI = [0.31, 0.92] (n = 12, p = 0.004).",
            "page": 26
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Many of the largest daily polarization peaks coincided with political or public health events in the two countries, and the average spike rose and subsided within days. Examples include Biden receiving his first vaccine dose and a Toronto protest against COVID-19 measures.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "we find that many of the largest polarization peaks coincide with\nhighly contentious events specific to each country’s context.",
            "page": 17
          },
          {
            "text": "The second-largest\npeak occurred on December 22, 2020, the day after Biden received his first COVID-19 vaccine [54].",
            "page": 18
          },
          {
            "text": "In Canada’s lockdown discourse (Fig 13B), the highest peak occurred on October 17, 2020, coinciding with a large\nToronto anti-mask protest against COVID-19 measures.",
            "page": 18
          },
          {
            "text": "This aggregate analysis (Fig\n14c) confirms a rapid, symmetric rise-and-decay profile around polarization peaks, occurring on timescales of days.",
            "page": 19
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Conservative users posted about conspiracy theories more often than progressive users in both countries. Nonetheless, daily conspiracy-related tweet volume had a small negative correlation with aggregate polarization in the United States and no significant correlation in Canada.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "show that conservative partisans in both countries tweet conspiracy-related content at higher rates than progressive\npartisans.",
            "page": 21
          },
          {
            "text": "In the United States (B), we find there is a statistically significant correlation −0.247 with\nCI=[−0.448,-0.023] (n = 88, p = 0.031) between these measures. In Canada (D), we find that there is no statistically significant correlation of 0.023 with\nCI=[−0.187,0.231] (n = 88, p = 0.831) between these measures.",
            "page": 22
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "scope_and_limits": [
      {
        "claim": "Semantic similarity is a proxy, not a direct measure of partisan polarization; the embeddings can miss signals relevant to the underlying divide.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "First, we measured partisan polarization only through the proxy of semantic similarity, which may in certain cases obscure signals not captured by semantic\nembedding representations.",
            "page": 24
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Averaging messages into user-level representations captures broad alignment rather than the full meaning of individual tweets; it can make sarcasm and indirect disagreement harder to detect.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Because our measure aggregates tweets into user-level embeddings, it captures\noverall partisan alignment at the user level, rather than fine-grained nuances in individual tweets. As a result, subtle rhetorical strategies such as sarcasm or indirect disagreement may be less apparent, while consistent linguistic cues, including negation, are preserved in the aggregated representation.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The analysis cannot reliably distinguish each Canadian party separately, and it excludes Bloc Québécois supporters and supporters of other minor parties.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Specifically for Canada, we found that the MLP could not distinguish the parties sufficiently",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "We also\nremoved supporters of other minor parties and the Bloc Quebecois.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Inferred affiliation is not definitive: self-reported information may be inaccurate, voter records may not reflect current online leanings, and name-based matching can miss users.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Additionally, our classification into partisan groups relies\non self-reported information, which may not be entirely accurate.",
            "page": 24
          },
          {
            "text": "voter registrations may be outdated or not fully reflect\ncurrent partisan leanings expressed online. We note that name-based matching can miss users due to nicknames, misspellings, or common names;",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The findings describe a politically engaged and often more partisan Twitter population, not a representative sample of American or Canadian public opinion.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Third, our analysis is based on Twitter (X) data, which may not fully capture the views and sentiments of the broader\nAmerican and Canadian public. Twitter users are more politically engaged and often more partisan than the general population, which means our results reflect dynamics within a vocal subset of online political discourse.",
            "page": 24
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The English-only analysis leaves out French-language discussion and may bias estimates in Canada, especially in Quebec.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Fourth, we restricted\nour analysis to English-language tweets, which in Canada means we primarily captured perspectives of either anglophones or bilingual francophones — a potential source of bias. For example, the high polarization observed in Quebec\nregarding COVID-19 measures may be influenced by this language limitation.",
            "page": 24
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Geographic assignment can be wrong when users provide incomplete, inaccurate, or deliberately misleading locations; VPNs and fake accounts add possible error.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Fifth, geographic attribution of users depends on self-reported location fields processed through tools such as Open\nStreet Map. While this is a reasonable approach, it is subject to inaccuracies if users provide false, incomplete, or",
            "page": 24
          },
          {
            "text": "humorous locations. Furthermore, the use of VPNs or fake accounts (including bots) may introduce additional error.",
            "page": 25
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Bots can also distort the polarization score itself by producing or amplifying partisan messages and widening the apparent linguistic divide.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Automated accounts can contribute disproportionately to partisan discourse by generating large volumes of ideologically\nslanted content or amplifying existing narratives, potentially inflating the apparent semantic distance between political\ngroups.",
            "page": 25
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Some apparent differences between countries may reflect different collection filters: the U.S. list emphasized elections, whereas the Canadian list explicitly included province names and COVID-related terms.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The U.S. keyword\nlist was dominated by election-related terms, then filtered for COVID-19 terms. By contrast, the Canadian keyword list\nincorporated province names and explicit COVID-related identifiers, which likely offered stronger pandemic relevance.",
            "page": 25
          },
          {
            "text": "This asymmetry may have contributed to cross-national differences in patterns of observed polarization, and this should\nbe considered when interpreting the comparative results.",
            "page": 25
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The late-2020 discourse and later vaccination rates were not measured at the same time. Their association is suggestive rather than proof that online polarization affected subsequent uptake.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Seventh, there are temporal mismatches between our Twitter dataset (collected in late 2020) and regional vaccination rates, which occurred primarily in 2021. As a result, correlations between polarization in vaccine-related discussions\nand later vaccination uptake should be understood as suggestive rather than causative.",
            "page": 25
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors draw no firm conclusion from the Canadian vaccination correlation because it is based on few regions; their account of how mandates might explain it is conjectural.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "While we get strong positive correlation with vaccination rate,\nit is over a relatively low number of points.",
            "page": 26
          },
          {
            "text": "We assume that vaccine partisan polarization increases, as people are not happy with being\nforced to be vaccinated, but most of the population still are vaccinated. However, with the few points, we do not have\na definite conclusion for this result.",
            "page": 26
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The correlation analyses do not isolate causal mechanisms, and the fact that a peak coincides with an event does not establish that the event caused it.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Finally, while several of our analyses rely on correlations, these results do not imply causation. The relationship\nbetween polarization and public health measures is complex and multidimensional, influenced by numerous social, political, and cultural factors that extend beyond the scope of our computational analysis.",
            "page": 25
          },
          {
            "text": "While direct causal evidence is not available, we find that many of the largest polarization peaks coincide with\nhighly contentious events specific to each country’s context.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "State-level examples suggest possible explanations for higher polarization in conservative regions but do not establish one explanation for the overall relationship.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "While our analysis did not identify a single explanation for the relationship between conservative politics and higher\npolarization, state-specific trajectories provide insights.",
            "page": 23
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study does not quantify the precise impact of social networks on polarization and subsequent health outcomes, or resolve how cross-border information flows shape polarization.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Additionally, the role of cross-border\ninformation flows in shaping polarization deserves further investigation",
            "page": 26
          },
          {
            "text": "social networks clearly contributed to the diffusion of partisan opinions during the COVID-19 pandemic, but quantifying\ntheir precise impact on polarization and subsequent public health outcomes remains an important challenge for future\nwork.",
            "page": 26
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "theoretical_positioning": [
      {
        "claim": "The paper extends computational research that measures polarization with contextual text embeddings. It treats embeddings as complementary to dictionary methods and chooses a text-only design for accessibility and scale, rather than rejecting network methods as ineffective.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Embedding-based models\ncapture latent semantic distinctions between ideological groups, providing methodological advantages that complement\ntraditional lexicon- or dictionary-based strategies.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "At the same time, network-based approaches such as inferring ideology\nfrom follower or retweet structures have proven highly effective in prior work [37,38,39].",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "Consequently,\nour study adopts a strictly text-based method, which remains both accessible and scalable for analyzing large-scale digital\ndiscourse.",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors build on research linking conservative political leaning to greater partisan division over COVID-19, extending it by examining how the divide varies within countries, changes over time, and coincides with specific events.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "In line with previous research ((e.g., [17,58–60])), we found that more right leaning states and provinces exhibited\ngreater partisan divisions around COVID-19 on Twitter (X), particularly concerning mask mandates and vaccine\ndistribution. However, we extended beyond these studies to characterize the geographic heterogeneity and temporal\nevolution of polarization, connecting features in our polarization metric to real-world events.",
            "page": 22
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "interlocutor_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Eric Merkley and colleagues identified a rare early Canadian cross-party consensus on COVID-19 emergency measures. Yang and colleagues qualify its reach: that consensus was not mirrored on social media, where conspiracy theories circulated widely.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Indeed, there was a rare “crosspartisan consensus” among Canadians regarding emergency measures in the early stages of the pandemic [103]. This\nconsensus, however, was not mirrored on social media, where conspiracy theories widely circulated [24,95].",
            "page": 24
          },
          {
            "text": "Merkley E, Bridgman A, Loewen PJ, Owen T, Ruths D, Zhilin O. A rare moment of cross-partisan consensus: Elite and public response to the\nCOVID-19 pandemic in Canada.",
            "page": 31
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
